<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · disaster</p><h1 class="enc-title">4.2-kiloyear event</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2200–2150 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/23rd-century-bce">23rd century BCE</a> · Also known as 4.2-kiloyear climate event</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The 4.2-kiloyear event was a period of severe aridification and climatic disruption around 2200–2150 BCE that affected much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> and other regions. Reduced rainfall undermined rain-fed agriculture in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and contributed to population movements and settlement abandonment. Historians frequently cite it as one of the environmental pressures behind the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/4-2-kiloyear-event.jpg" alt="4.2-kiloyear event" width="320" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jianjun Wang, Liguang Sun, Liqi Chen, Libin Xu, Yuhong Wang &amp; Xinming Wang · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Global_distribution_of_the_Bond_Holocene_IRD_Event_3_also_named_4.2_kiloyear_event._The_hatched_areas_were_affected_by_wet_conditions_or_flooding_and_the_dotted_areas_by_drought_or_dust_storms.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
